WEBVTT

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:06.340
Mr. Hopkins’ bookstore was a quiet island in the noisy stream of the modern city.
霍普金斯先生的书店是喧嚣的现代都市洪流中一座宁静的小岛。

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:10.170
For decades, it had been a shelter for book lovers.
几十年来，它一直是爱书人的避风港。

00:00:10.970 --> 00:00:16.020
The wooden sign above the door, weathered but proud, read “Hopkins’ Books” in brick red.
门上方的木招牌虽已饱经风霜，却依然昂然挺立，上面用砖红色写着“霍普金斯书店”。

00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:21.020
It had been a constant place where time moved slower with the first turn of a page.
它一直是一个始终如一的地方，在这里，书页一翻开，时间就仿佛慢了下来。

00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:22.980
Now, it was closing.
如今，它要关门了。

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:30.760
The news felt like a physical blow to David, a quiet college student who had grown up reading in the cozy corners of the shop.
这个消息对戴维来说就像当头挨了一拳。他是个文静的大学生，从小就窝在这家店温馨的角落里读书长大。

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:35.990
The smell of old paper was the smell of his childhood, witnessing his growth.
旧书纸的气味就是他童年的气味，见证着他的成长。

00:00:36.790 --> 00:00:40.250
He couldn’t imagine the street without its warm, inviting light.
他无法想象，这条街上没有了它那温暖诱人的灯光会是什么样子。

00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:46.140
When the news spread throughout the entire neighborhood, worry was written all over everyone’s faces.
消息传遍了整个街区，每个人都满脸忧虑。

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:50.000
The reason for the closure was a woman named Sarah Chen.
书店关门是因为一个名叫莎拉·陈的女人。

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:55.830
Representing a large development company, she had made a generous offer to buy the building.
她代表一家大型开发公司，出高价要买下这栋楼。

00:00:56.630 --> 00:01:03.220
She saw the space not for its shelves of stories, but for its potential as a trendy café, a profitable upgrade.
她看中这个地方，不是因为那一排排装满故事的书架，而是因为它有潜力改成一家时髦的咖啡馆，实现一次有利可图的升级。

00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:06.160
To her, it was just business.
对她来说，这只是一桩生意。

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:10.920
Mr. Hopkins, now old and tired, felt defeated.
霍普金斯先生如今年迈体乏，感到心灰意冷。

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:17.700
“Maybe it’s time, David,” he sighed, his hand resting on a dusty copy of his favorite novel.
“也许是时候了，戴维。”他叹了口气，一只手搭在一本积满灰尘的书上——那是他最喜欢的小说。

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:19.810
“The world has moved on.”
“世界已经变了。”

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:38.070
On the day of the final meeting, David arrived early, his backpack filled with items he had collected over the years from the store: a bookmark from his tenth birthday, a receipt from the first book he had bought with his own money, a photograph of himself and Mr. Hopkins taken during a summer reading program.
最后一次会谈那天，戴维早早来了，背包里装满了他多年来从书店收集的物品：一枚十岁生日时得到的书签，一张他用自己的钱买第一本书时的收据，还有一张参加暑期阅读活动时他和霍普金斯先生的合影。

00:01:38.870 --> 00:01:44.290
He walked to the children’s section and drew out a small, worn copy of The Adventures of Tom Sawyer.
他走到儿童区，抽出一本小巧而破旧的《汤姆·索亚历险记》。

00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:49.500
The cover was faded, the pages yellowed, but the spine still held strong.
封面已经褪色，书页也已发黄，可书脊依然结实。

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:53.090
Sarah arrived precisely at 2:00 p.m.
莎拉下午两点准时到达。

00:01:53.890 --> 00:01:59.040
She wasted no time getting down to business, reviewing the contract with Mr. Hopkins.
她一刻也没耽搁，马上谈起正事，和霍普金斯先生逐条审阅合同。

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:03.360
David watched from the sidelines, his heart pounding in his chest.
戴维在一旁看着，心在胸口怦怦直跳。

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:07.810
He couldn’t let this happen—not without trying to make his voice heard.
他不能让这件事就这样发生——至少得试着发出自己的声音。

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:14.440
A decision was made to combine a bookstore and a café in one project, and the construction began.
最终的决定是把书店和咖啡馆合并为一个项目，随后工程动工。
